{"amendments":[{"body":"Blocking: this patch does not implement `tanksim-6e5.5.6`'s geometry-scaling work. Geometry scaling still only changes dimensions and substrate nutrient stores in `crates/tank_scenarios/src/lib.rs:238-257`. The startup defaults that the bead calls out remain fixed: `startup_defaults_for_scenario()` still hard-codes shrimp counts per scenario in `crates/tank_scenarios/src/lib.rs:289-320`, `apply_startup_overrides()` still falls back to a fixed `200.0` LPH filter and never scales heater wattage in `crates/tank_scenarios/src/lib.rs:672-708`, and `build_plant_guilds()` still seeds every guild at `5.0` g in `crates/tank_scenarios/src/lib.rs:785-821`. `crates/tank_core/src/types/hardware.rs:64-91` also still carries the fixed `50.0` W heater / `200.0` LPH filter defaults. The actual edits are unrelated alkalinity-accounting changes in `crates/tank_core/src/types/budget.rs:68-109`, `crates/tank_core/src/systems/nitrogen_cycle.rs:12-23`, and `crates/tank_core/src/systems/water_change.rs:131-152`, so the requested behavior and acceptance criteria around geometry-scaled hardware, plant biomass, and stocking are still unmet.","rationale":"As submitted, this would merge a change for a different problem while leaving the target bead unresolved."},{"body":"Robustness gap: the new alkalinity budget axis is not covered by the engine's invariant guard. `crates/tank_core/src/types/budget.rs:68-109` makes alkalinity part of the canonical budget model, and `crates/tank_core/src/systems/water_change.rs:131-152` now emits explicit alkalinity deltas, but `crates/tank_core/src/engine.rs:845-868` still validates only nitrogen and carbon before accepting a tick. That means an incorrect explicit alkalinity delta can pass `step_hours()` silently and only be caught if a caller happens to assert `Element::Alkalinity` separately. If alkalinity is meant to be first-class budget bookkeeping, the same guard that protects N/C should protect alkalinity too.","rationale":"Without extending the guard, the new accounting path is much easier to regress than the existing tracked elements."}],"summary":"Reviewed the diff, read the changed budget/nitrogen/water-change code plus the relevant scenario-default code, and ran targeted `tank_core` tests. The alkalinity-accounting edits are internally consistent, but I found one blocking correctness issue: the branch does not implement the requested geometry-scaling work at all. I also found one robustness gap: the new alkalinity budget path is not enforced by the engine's tracked-budget guard."}